Folliculitis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Folliculocentric pustules, Erythematous halos, Hair-bearing distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-019
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a painful, widespread, pruritic pustular eruption on the legs and buttocks following heavy physical exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, rapidly spreading pustular rash over the lower half of the body accompanied by severe itch and mild pain, leading to significant distress and sleep disruption. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for extensive staphylococcal infection, rule out widespread herpes simplex or a severe cutaneous adverse drug reaction, and initiate targeted topical and oral therapies before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs and backside are covered in hundreds of painful, itchy white pimples. It flared up completely out of nowhere after my long run yesterday."
History of Present Illness: The patient developed a widespread eruption of multiple small erythematous papules and pustules 24 hours ago. The rash is exquisitely pruritic and mildly tender, localized predominantly to hair-bearing areas subject to friction, specifically the bilateral thighs, calves, and buttocks. The patient reports wearing tight, occlusive athletic leggings during a long-distance run in humid conditions 48 hours ago, leading to heavy sweating. Over the last 12 hours, some pustules have spontaneously ruptured, leaving tiny crusts. The patient denies any fever, systemic malaise, or recent hot tub use. The severe itch has prevented sleep.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme discomfort and sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight frequently while sitting, clearly distressed by the pruritus.
Physical Exam: Examination of the lower extremities and buttocks reveals hundreds of discrete, 1-3 mm folliculocentric pustules. Each individual pustule is centered perfectly around a hair follicle and is surrounded by a 1-2 mm distinct erythematous halo. The intervening skin between the pustules is entirely normal. There are scattered tiny, honey-colored crusts where pustules have been excoriated. There are no deep, tender follicular nodules, no confluent areas of cellulitic erythema, no fluctuance, and no regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Athletic gear visible in the laundry; patient confirms wearing tight lycra.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Widespread Acute Mechanical/Bacterial Folliculitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pseudomonas Folliculitis ("Hot Tub" Folliculitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lack of recent hot tub or unchlorinated pool exposure, and the distribution heavily favoring frictional areas rather than standard swimsuit occlusion zones.

Acne Vulgaris - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyper-acute onset over 24 hours, the presence of exclusively monomorphic pustules without comedones, and the atypical distribution on the calves and buttocks.

Systemic Infection/Bacteremia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of systemic symptoms (fever, chills, tachycardia) and the strictly superficial, hair-follicle-centered nature of the lesions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, extensive case of folliculitis, clearly triggered by a combination of occlusion, friction, and sweat (athletic gear). Triage appropriately recognized the widespread, rapid spread and severe pruritus as warranting assessment to rule out a more insidious infectious or allergic etiology. Clinical examination confirms a superficial, localized inflammation of the hair follicles without deep abscess formation or systemic involvement. Providing immediate diagnosis, prescribing potent topical antibacterials and symptomatic relief for the pruritus addressed the patient's acute distress and sleep deprivation, safely deferring further management to their routine GP.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Mupirocin 2% ointment to be applied to the most inflamed areas twice daily. Recommended use of an antibacterial wash (e.g., Triclosan or Chlorhexidine 1%) in the shower. Advised an oral antihistamine (Cetirizine 10mg) for symptomatic itch relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: By exclusively wearing loose, breathable cotton clothing for the next week, you will remove the friction and trapped sweat that caused the infection, allowing the hair follicles to breathe and heal rapidly. By washing immediately after any future exercise and changing out of damp gym clothes instantly, you will mechanically remove the bacteria and sweat before they can invade the pores again.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days if the eruption has not significantly improved.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the small pustules merge together to form large, deeply painful, hot red lumps, or if you develop a fever and chills, present to a medical centre or Emergency Department promptly.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Folliculitis is highly recurrent if the mechanical triggers (tight clothing, heavy sweat) are not altered.
Clinical Course: The intense itching and small whiteheads should dry up and resolve over the next 3-5 days with the antibacterial wash and avoidance of tight clothing.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor for any lumps growing larger than a pea, becoming intensely painful, or spreading deep redness, which indicates a deeper boil forming.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tight, sweaty clothing damaged the hair follicles and pushed normal skin bacteria deep into the pores, causing hundreds of tiny superficial infections. The antibacterial wash will clear the surface bacteria, and the cream will treat the worst spots.
Actionable Steps: Avoid shaving the affected areas until completely healed to prevent further spreading. Wash all athletic gear in hot water.

SMS INFORMATION

